  "worked_solution": "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{25-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{25-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $5$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<5.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{25-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{25-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{25-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{25-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 25-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ l & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},5)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $4$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\sqrt{25-{(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
